    {
      "id": "normal",
      "kind": "normal",
      "title": "Normal: the bilirubin journey",
      "start": 20,
      "end": 60,
      "altText": "A pathway: hemoglobin → heme → biliverdin → unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin, water-insoluble, carried on albumin → liver adds glucuronic acid (conjugated, direct, water-soluble) → bile. Note: the diazo reaction is the reference method; “direct” bilirubin reacts without an accelerator, “indirect” needs one.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "n1",
          "start": 20,
          "end": 30,
          "text": "Bilirubin comes mostly from hemoglobin breakdown. Heme becomes biliverdin, then unconjugated bilirubin."
        },
        {
          "id": "n2",
          "start": 30,
          "end": 40,
          "text": "Unconjugated bilirubin doesn’t dissolve in water, so it travels on albumin to the liver."
        },
        {
          "id": "n3",
          "start": 40,
          "end": 50,
          "text": "The liver attaches glucuronic acid — conjugation — making it water-soluble, so it can enter bile."
        },
        {
          "id": "n4",
          "start": 50,
          "end": 60,
          "text": "Labs measure these with the diazo reaction. Conjugated bilirubin reacts directly; unconjugated needs an accelerator. That’s where “direct” and “indirect” come from."
        }
      ],
