    {
      "id": "change",
      "kind": "change",
      "title": "What changes: hepatocellular or cholestatic?",
      "start": 60,
      "end": 104,
      "altText": "Two enzyme families: hepatocellular injury releases ALT and AST; cholestasis raises ALP (with GGT) and often bilirubin. The R value = (ALT ÷ ULN) ÷ (ALP ÷ ULN): > 5 hepatocellular, 2–5 mixed, < 2 cholestatic. GGT confirms a liver source of high ALP — it isn’t raised by bone disease (but drugs, alcohol and other organs can raise it). AST:ALT > 2 is typical of alcoholic hepatitis.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "start": 60,
          "end": 70,
          "text": "Two patterns. Hepatocellular injury releases the aminotransferases, ALT and AST. Cholestasis raises alkaline phosphatase, often with bilirubin."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "start": 70,
          "end": 81,
          "text": "The R value sorts them: ALT over its upper limit, divided by ALP over its upper limit. Above five is hepatocellular, below two cholestatic, in between mixed."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "start": 81,
          "end": 93,
          "text": "ALP also comes from bone and placenta. A raised GGT points to a liver or biliary source — bone disease doesn’t raise it — though drugs and alcohol can."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4",
          "start": 93,
          "end": 104,
          "text": "And an AST-to-ALT ratio above two is typical of alcoholic hepatitis."
        }
      ],
