    {
      "id": "lab",
      "kind": "lab",
      "title": "In the lab: delta-bilirubin and synthetic function",
      "start": 104,
      "end": 144,
      "altText": "Delta-bilirubin: conjugated bilirubin covalently bound to albumin; measured as direct bilirubin (cannot be distinguished from conjugated in routine assays); takes on albumin’s half-life of 17–21 days vs 2–4 h for free conjugated bilirubin; too big to be filtered, so no bilirubinuria — explains prolonged direct hyperbilirubinemia during recovery. Synthetic function: albumin falls slowly (long half-life) and also drops in inflammation; PT/INR rises when the liver can’t make enough clotting factors.",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": "l1",
          "start": 104,
          "end": 114,
          "text": "Delta-bilirubin is conjugated bilirubin that has bonded permanently to albumin. Routine assays count it as direct bilirubin — they can’t tell it apart."
        },
        {
          "id": "l2",
          "start": 114,
          "end": 124,
          "text": "It inherits albumin’s half-life — seventeen to twenty-one days, versus two to four hours for free conjugated bilirubin. And it’s too large to enter urine."
        },
        {
          "id": "l3",
          "start": 124,
          "end": 134,
          "text": "That’s our patient: a slowly falling direct bilirubin with no bilirubinuria during recovery — usually not a sign of ongoing failure."
        },
        {
          "id": "l4",
          "start": 134,
          "end": 144,
          "text": "For synthetic function, albumin falls slowly because of its long half-life, and it drops with inflammation too. PT and INR rise when the liver can’t make enough clotting factors."
        }
      ],
